{
  "raw": {
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/b174ad4128c2460a682bbf69d63e12867fb86b080": {
      "title": "Call for Doctoral applications open",
      "description": "Call for Doctoral applications open at the Graduate School Life Science Munich (LSM) (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4542 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Biology - Graduate School Life Science Munich (LSM) Remuneration group TV-L Full-time / Part-time Part-time (65%) Start date As soon as possible (limited to 3-4 years) About us: The Graduate School Life Science Munich (LSM) is an international doctoral programme at one of Europe's leading research universities — Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München (LMU). The programme spans a wide range of disciplines including Biochemistry, Cell and Developmental Biology, Ecology, Genetics, Microbiology, Plant Sciences, Zoology, and more. Doctoral candidates join a vibrant, diverse, and truly international research community at the LMU Biocenter in Planegg-Martinsried. We are looking for you: Call for Doctoral applications open at the Graduate School Life Science Munich (LSM) (m/f/x) in Planegg Your tasks and responsibilities: The LSM calls for doctoral applications on a yearly basis. Available Positions This year, two pathways are available: ________________________________________ 1. Doctoral Positions The following doctoral positions are currently available. Please note that for all positions listed below, funding is still in the process of being secured. Supervisors are committed to supporting promising candidates in identifying and applying for appropriate funding — such as a fellowship, scholarship, or third-party funding. Antisense oligonucleotide treatment for MAST3-associated epilepsy — Prof. David Keays, Sensory and Developmental Neuroscience Avian Cerebral Organoids — Prof. David Keays, Sensory and Developmental Neuroscience Identification and Characterisation of Novel Microtubule Associated Proteins — Prof. David Keays, Sensory and Developmental Neuroscience Transcriptional networks shaping the legume root symbiosis with nitrogen-fixing rhizobia — Prof. Martin Parniske, Plant Genetics Harnessing natural genetic resources to defend fruits against insect attack — Prof. Martin Parniske, Plant Genetics For full project descriptions, please visit here . ________________________________________ 2. Independent Research Proposals (new this year) A number of LSM faculty members are open to receiving original research proposals in specific areas. Rather than applying to a pre-defined project, candidates develop their own original proposal inspired by the research interests of a participating faculty member. Should a proposal be of interest to the potential supervisor, they would be willing to support the candidate in identifying and applying for appropriate funding — such as a fellowship, scholarship, or third-party funding. Participating faculty members and their research areas: Prof. Claude Becker — Epigenetics, epigenomics, plant-microbe interactions, biosynthetic gene clusters Prof. Alexander Keller — Biodiversity, AI and computer vision, plant-pollinator interactions, ecological genomics Prof. Dario Leister — Regulation and improvement of photosynthesis (open to CSC candidates only) Prof. Michael Matschiner — Phylogenetics, adaptive radiation, museum and fossil specimens Dr. Natascha Turetzek — Arthropod evo-devo, gene duplication, sensory organ diversification, thermal stress Full research area abstracts can be viewed here . Your qualifications: Master's degree (MSc) or equivalent in biology, biochemistry, bioinformatics, biophysics, biotechnology, medicine, or a related discipline In exceptional cases, a 4-year Bachelor's degree with Honours (min. 240 ECTS, with written thesis) is accepted Proficiency in written and spoken English The most recent final examination must have taken place no more than four years prior to the date of application There are no tuition fees Benefits: An international, multidisciplinary doctoral programme at a world-class research institution Individual mentoring and a structured Thesis Advisory Committee (TAC) A wide range of transferable skills workshops, seminars, and career development opportunities Financial support for conferences and scientific events A vibrant student community at the LMU Biocenter The remunaration is based on the German public sector pay scale (TV-L). In addition, you can benefit from the various LMU corporate benefits . People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: Further information and details about the online application process, requirements, eligibility and recruiting faculty can be found here . The deadline for applications is 19.10.2026. Apply now through our online application portal . Should you have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact us! Graduate School Life Science Munich Nadine Hamze LMU Munich, Faculty of Biology Grosshadernerstr. 2 82152 Planegg-Martinsried, Germany Where knowledge is everything. LMU researchers work at the highest level on the great questions affecting people, society, culture, the environment and technology — supported by experts in administration, IT and tech. Become part of LMU Munich! In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/220a57d19e567024bdbd0748def3095a62a556ef0": {
      "title": "Doctoral Researcher in Assyriology/Ancient History",
      "description": "Doctoral Researcher in Assyriology/Ancient History - ERC Project: “Rewriting the End of Cuneiform Civilization” (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4585 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty for the Study of Culture - Institute for Assyriology and Hittitology Remuneration group TV-L E13 Full-time / Part-time Part-time (65%) Start date 2026-12-01 limited to 2030-08-31 About us: The Institute for Assyriology and Hittitology is a leading center for cutting-edge research in Digital Assyriology, with a strong focus on the digital preservation and analysis of cuneiform sources. The ERC-funded project “Rewriting the End of Cuneiform Civilization” (RECC) aims to revolutionize our understanding of the final centuries of cuneiform culture through cutting-edge digital humanities and machine learning techniques. The project will develop tools for paleographic analysis, automatic dating of cuneiform tablets, and the study of scribal practices in Hellenistic and Parthian Babylonia. We are looking for you: Doctoral Researcher in Assyriology/Ancient History - ERC Project: “Rewriting the End of Cuneiform Civilization” (m/f/x) in Munich Your tasks and responsibilities: Write a dissertation on Localism and Cosmopolitanism in Hellenistic/Parthian Babylonia , analyzing textual production and civic identity. Contribute to the Catalogue and Editions of the project. Your qualifications: Master’s degree in Assyriology, Ancient History or a related field with top grades. Experience reading cuneiform tablets . Familiarity with digital tools (e.g., eBL platform, ATF transliteration). Fluency in English; German is a plus. Benefits: The opportunity to be part of a dynamic, internationally connected team working at the forefront of Digital Assyriology. Career development through the LMU qualification program. A stimulating working environment at one of Germany’s top-ranked universities. Our institute is located in the heart of the vibrant and internationally diverse city of Munich with excellent public transportation links. Organizational assistance from the LMU Gateway service for relocation to Munich. Collaboration with Assyriologists, ML experts, and international partners . Flexible working hours. People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: Submit your CV, academic transcripts, writing sample (e.g. MA thesis), and motivation letter (1–2 pages) as a single PDF  to: sekretariat.jimenez@assyr.fak12.uni-muenchen.de Subject: Application for PhD2 (RECC Project) Deadline for applications is October 16th, 2026 Where knowledge is everything. LMU researchers work at the highest level on the great questions affecting people, society, culture, the environment and technology — supported by experts in administration, IT and tech. Become part of LMU Munich! In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/bffa5ed286f9d2d67eae0d72868f5688f04df6040": {
      "title": "Doctoral Researcher (m/f/x)",
      "description": "Doctoral Researcher  (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4601 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Psychology and Educational Sciences - Faculty of Psychology and educational Science Chair of Education and Educational Psychology Remuneration group TV-L E13 Full-time / Part-time Part-time (75%) Start date 01.01.2027 (3 years) About us: The DFG-funded Collaborative Research Centre SHARP ‘Simulation-based learning in higher education: Advancing research on process diagnostics and personalised interventions’ contributes to theory-building on personalising learning using simulations in higher education. As a joint research context, SHARP focuses on diagnosing and intervening as two highly relevant professional practices in many academic professions. The subproject P ‘Advancing Person Centred Methods for Research on Simulation-Based Learning in Higher Education’, explores the statistical approach of mixture modeling (e.g., latent profile analysis, latent transition-, growth mixture- and hidden Markov-modeling) as a means for modeling for whom which experimental treatment works best to support simulationbased learning and how to adapt to learners` preconditions. The project is currently looking for a doctoral researcher. The position is located at the Department of Psychology, Chair of Education and Educational Psychology, Professorship for Methods of Empirical Educa-tional Research (Prof. Peter Edelsbrunner), in collabo-ration with the Professorship of Research on Learning and Instruction (Prof. Sarah Hofer) and the Professorship Psychology of Teaching and Learning at the School of Social Sciences and Technology of TUM (Prof. Doris Holzberger). You can find an overview of all sub-projects and open positions here . We are looking for you: Doctoral Researcher  (m/f/x) in Munich Your tasks and responsibilities: planning, preparing and conducting methodological and applied research on mixture modelling in simulationbased learning conducting systematic reviews, statistical simulation studies, conceptual methodological research, and applied statistical modelling analysing data contributing to scientific publications. Your qualifications: You have a very good Master´s degree or equivalent in the field of psychology, educational science, applied statistics, data science, or learning sciences. You are interested in interdisciplinary collaboration and working in research teams. You have very good knowledge of social science research methods including the R software environment, statistical analyses and modelling. You interested in planning and conducting studies at the crosssection of quantitative methodology and applied educational research. Experiences with mixture modelling and process analyses would be an advantage. You have very good written and spoken English skills. Benefits: We offer the opportunity to gain further academic qualifications, particularly to obtain a doctorate in educational psychology, educational research or learning sciences. Contributing to the CRC SHARP, you will become part of an interdisciplinary group of researchers working in an excellent research environment to significantly advance higher education theory and practice. The CRC provides all its members with tailored support, coaching and supervision, including special support for international researchers. You will be paid an attractive salary at the payscale 0.75 FTE of TV-L E13. Your contract will be limited to 3 years. Your workplace is centrally located in Munich and easily accessible by public transportation. LMU has signed the \"Diversity Charter\" and is committed to diversity among its employees. We actively promote gender equality. We welcome applications from women and other groups that are currently underrepresented in the academic system. People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: Your detailed application includes a cover letter, your CV and copies of your transcripts and certificates. Please submit your application by 19/10/2026 at the latest. If you have any questions, please contact Prof. Peter Edelsbrunner ( peter.edelsbrunner@lmu.de ). Where knowledge is everything. LMU researchers work at the highest level on the great questions affecting people, society, culture, the environment and technology — supported by experts in administration, IT and tech. Become part of LMU Munich! In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/7fce5b8e18c3c7c18a1acef1d75a70bb08d1e9cd0": {
      "title": "Full Professorship (W3) of Internal Medicine/Pneumology (Chair)",
      "description": "Full Professorship (W3) of Internal Medicine/Pneumology (Chair) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4551 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Medicine Remuneration group BesGr W3 Start date commencing on 1 April 2027 Full Professorship (W3) of Internal Medicine/Pneumology (Chair) As one of Europe’s leading research universities, Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) in Munich is committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Building on its more than 500-year-long tradition, it offers a broad spectrum that covers all areas of knowledge within its 18 Faculties, ranging from the humanities, law, economics and social sciences, to medicine and the natural sciences. The Faculty of Medicine invites applications for a Full Professorship (W3) of Internal Medicine/Pneumology (Chair) commencing on 1 April 2027. The professorship will be appointed to the chairmanship of the Department of Medicine V (Pneumology) at the University Hospital of LMU Munich and represents the subject in its entirety in research, teaching and patient care. The LMU University Hospital is one of the largest and most renowned of its kind in Germany and holds top positions nationally and internationally in patient care and research. The focus of the professorship should be on translational pneumology, which will be further developed and expanded in close cooperation with the Institute of Experimental Pneumology, LMU University Hospital and the Institute of Lung Health and Immunity of the Helmholtz Center Munich. The Department of Medicine V is part of the Comprehensive Pneumology Center Munich (CPC-M), the German Center for Lung Research (DZL), the Comprehensive Cancer Center Munich (CCC-M) and the Bavarian Cancer Research Center (BZKF) as well as numerous national and international research networks. Cutting-edge research and participation in the numerous research initiatives of the faculty and the German health centers are expected. In addition, leadership experience in a clinical setting and a strong willingness for interdisciplinary collaboration and shared use of resources with the other internal medicine departments and thoracic surgery are expected. Experience in the field of transplant medicine is desirable. Prerequisites for this position are a university degree, a doctoral degree or a comparable specific qualification, a board certification as a specialist in internal medicine with a focus on pneumology, teaching skills at university level, excellent academic achievements and a productive and promising research program. The clinical duties and responsibilities of this professorship, including the billing of private patients, shall be reimbursed under the terms of a “Chefarztvertrag” (chairmanship contract) with a private-law contract for the professorship. This regulation can, however, potentially be disregarded if a professor has already been a chair of a department in a fully tenured professorship (C4-/W3-status) with the authorization to bill private patients. LMU Munich makes a point of providing newly appointed professors with various types of support, such as welcoming services and assistance for dual career couples. LMU Munich is an equal opportunity employer. The University continues to be very successful in increasing the number of female faculty members and strongly encourages applications from female candidates. LMU Munich intends to enhance the diversity of its faculty members. People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: Please submit your application (curriculum vitae, scientific career, references, certificates, a list of publications and lectures, external funds) as well as a short application form (see https://www.med.lmu.de/en/academic-career/professorship/ ) to the Dean of the Faculty of Medicine, Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München, Bavariaring 19. 80336 Munich, Germany, no later than October 8th 2026. In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/31f6f17bd30e3788553828ab08bdd8692499f7250": {
      "title": "ISI Junior Research Group Leader in Wealth Inequality Research (m/f/x)",
      "description": "ISI Junior Research Group Leader in Wealth Inequality Research (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4602 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Social Sciences - ISI - Munich International Stone Center for Inequality Research Remuneration group TV-L Full-time / Part-time Full-time (100%) Start date Between 1 April and 1 December 2027 (initially a 3-year position) About us: The Munich International Stone Center for Inequality Research (ISI) conducts interdisciplinary and internationally oriented research on social and economic inequality, with a particular focus on wealth inequality. It brings together the expertise available at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München, supports researchers at all career stages, and promotes exchange with leading research institutions in Germany and abroad. We are looking for a Junior Research Group Leader in Wealth Inequality Research to join and strengthen our research team. The ideal candidate holds a PhD in a social science discipline, has begun to build a research portfolio on wealth and will use this position to substantially advance the field of wealth inequality research. This advance can come from a variety of angles, such as new empirical work, theoretical development, or contributions to data infrastructure. A strong candidate will also share the international and interdisciplinary orientation that is at the heart of our research center. Since this position comes with funding to build a research group, the proposed project should be ambitious in scope. The position is expected to run for an initial three year period, with an option for extension upon review. The position should begin at some point in 2027. We are looking for you: ISI Junior Research Group Leader in Wealth Inequality Research (m/f/x) in Munich Your tasks and responsibilities: Produce cutting-edge research on wealth inequality; Establish and lead a junior research group; Supervise one to two doctoral candidates; Teach one seminar in your field per semester; Support ISI activities by, for example, evaluating applications for ISI fellowships or the ISI Pipeline Program; Actively contribute to ISI science communication. This includes communicating research findings clearly and accessibly to audiences beyond the academic community; Participate in ISI events, including our biannual ISI Wealth Conference. Your proposed research project Applicants should submit an ambitious and original proposal (max. 3 pages) for a junior research group that has the potential to advance the field of wealth inequality research significantly. The proposal should: Address a major unanswered question or emerging challenge in wealth inequality research and clearly explain the project’s innovative, potentially agenda-setting contribution; Present a coherent overarching research topic, including the project’s central research questions, theoretical approach, and expected scientific contribution; Outline distinct but closely connected individual research projects for the group leader and one to two doctoral candidates, explaining how the three projects contribute to the group’s overall research agenda; Describe the proposed data and methods, anticipated outputs, opportunities for interdisciplinary and international collaboration, and the feasibility of completing the research program within the funding period. Your qualifications: PhD in sociology, economics, or a related field, with a clear focus on wealth inequality research; Track record of high-quality publications; Strong team player with an interest in interdisciplinary work; Experience in science communication or a strong willingness to engage actively in this area; Ability to work independently and a high level of commitment and motivation; Excellent English skills; German-language ability is an advantage but not a requirement. Benefits: Remuneration is based on the provisions of the German public-sector collective agreement for the federal states ( TV-L ). Depending on the successful candidate’s qualifications and individual circumstances, the position will be classified at salary grade E 14 or E 15; A scientifically challenging and engaging position within an interdisciplinary, research-oriented team; Funding to establish a junior research group with up to two PhD positions and a research budget for travel and events; Excellent training and professional development opportunities; A centrally located, modern workplace in Munich that is easily accessible by public transport and offers innovative spaces for hosting different types of events; Flexible working hours and the option to work partly from home (please note that this position cannot be carried out remotely); A range of employee benefits , including family services and relocation assistance. Also possible in a part-time capacity. People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: Please submit your complete application exclusively via our online application tool by 15 November 2026, including the following documents (uploaded in one pdf file): Cover letter (max. 2 pages) CV Project description (max. 3 pages) Science communications approach (max. 1 page): Briefly describe specific ideas and your motivation for science communication activities for your project. If you have had the opportunity to gain such experiences, feel free to draw on examples of previous activities, such as media coverage, interviews, public talks, podcasts, or participation in public events and panel discussions. Contact information for two references Where knowledge is everything. LMU researchers work at the highest level on the great questions affecting people, society, culture, the environment and technology — supported by experts in administration, IT and tech. Become part of LMU Munich! In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal Online application"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/a9adb4abcb71e168aa1143b430c2f431e389a1b60": {
      "title": "PhD position in Immunology (m/f/x)",
      "description": "PhD position in Immunology (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4595 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Medicine - Biomedical Center Remuneration group TV-L E13 Full-time / Part-time Part-time (65%) Start date As soon as possible About us: The Institute for Immunology is part of the Biomedical Center (BMC) of the LMU Faculty of Medicine. Research in the Krug lab is dedicated to elucidating developmental and functional diversity of dendritic cell (DC) subpopulations in the context of viral infection and vaccination. We use yellow fever vaccination (YF17D) as a model of a highly efficient and long-lasting immune response in humans and want to understand how this response is initiated as a blueprint for development of effective anti-viral vaccines. We aim to define the early response to the vaccine virus in a human skin model and in mice. We ask which skin-resident cells at the injection site in the skin are infected and propagate the virus in context with the local type I IFN response and how dendritic cells get activated and present the viral antigens to T cells to promote optimal T cell differentiation. We are looking for you: PhD position in Immunology (m/f/x) in Planegg Your tasks and responsibilities: Design and perform experiments to study biological function of dendritic cells using in vitro and in vivo models Employ high-dimensional flow cytometry and multiplex immunofluorescence including computational analysis and data integration Keep up to date with project-related literature and contribute ideas to develop the research project Communicate and collaborate across disciplines and professional groups Actively participate in meetings, seminars and conferences Contribute to writing research manuscripts and reports Your qualifications: Master’s degree in Immunology, Molecular Medicine or related natural science disciplines Scientific background in Immunology and enthusiasm for innate immunity and dendritic cells Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, fluency in English (spoken and written) Organizational and team skills, flexibility, curiosity, responsible work ethics Experience in cellular biology, molecular biology and immunology techniques desirable Experience with multiparameter flow cytometry and/or multiparameter microscopy desirable Basic bioinformatics skills (R, Python) desirable Benefits: An exciting project in an innovative and modern laboratory Excellent training and support in a motivated, international team Excellent infrastructure and core facilities at the BMC Opportunity to obtain a PhD in Medical Research (structured program of the Munich Medical Research School ) Participation in a collaborative and interdisciplinary research project, participation in research consortia ( TRR237 , Yellow4Flavi ) Free parking and good public transportation connections Numerous LMU employee benefits, e.g. discounted fitness and cultural offers, job public transport ticket, Jobbike etc. Salary based on the TV-L scale (E13/65%) We welcome applications from all backgrounds. The promotion of diversity is an important strategic goal at our center. This is reflected in the BMC Code of Conduct , which actively promotes diversity and equal opportunities. Destination Germany Do you have questions about visa requirements or degree recognition? Are you looking for housing, childcare, or trying to decide on the best health insurance? The \" Research in Bavaria \" and LMU Gateway website provides help in these practical matters. People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: Please submit your complete application exclusively via our online application tool , including the following documents individually as pdf files: Cover letter describing briefly your motivation and qualification Curriculum Vitae (max. 2 pages) Short statement of your general research interests (approx. 200 words). What do you find interesting about our research and this PhD position ? (approx. 200 words). Contact details for at least two referees, including at least one direct supervisor who has worked with you for at least six months. AI Policy: AI assistance is welcome for tasks like proofreading, but you must clearly mention where it was used. Primarily AI-generated applications will be automatically discarded without feedback Applications will be considered if submitted by 16. October 2026 . Where knowledge is everything. LMU researchers work at the highest level on the great questions affecting people, society, culture, the environment and technology — supported by experts in administration, IT and tech. Become part of LMU Munich! In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal Online application"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/075aaa47cd85dd7cab0ae184c51464910f2c4c0d0": {
      "title": "PhD Researcher in Computational Social Science (m/f/x)",
      "description": "PhD Researcher in Computational Social Science (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4588 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Social Sciences - Geschwister-Scholl-Institute for Political Science, Unit Digitalization and Political Behavior (Prof. Dr. Alexander Wuttke) Remuneration group TV-L E13 Full-time / Part-time Part-time (75%) Start date 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible About us: At LMU Munich, the Unit Digitalization and Political Behavior (Prof. Dr. Alexander Wuttke) and Geschwister-Scholl Institute for Political Science are looking for a PhD Researcher in Computational Social Science. The position is fixed-term for 4 years, with the possibility of extension. We are looking for you: PhD Researcher in Computational Social Science (m/f/x) in Munich Your tasks and responsibilities: Are you excited by the idea of using large language models and text data to answer questions that matter for society? Do you want to understand how ordinary people think about democracy? If so, we warmly invite you to join our team. You will become part of the Emmy Noether Research Group \"Predictably Paradoxical: Mapping the Democratic Mind with AI\" ( Project Proposal ). The Emmy Noether group seeks to illuminate the often puzzling and contradictory beliefs and attitudes people hold about democracy. Standardized surveys struggle to map what is on people's minds because their response options only capture what researchers already expect. The group therefore uses large language models as interviewers in semi-structured interviews. With AI Conversational Interviewing, respondents in many countries describe in their own words what democracy means to them, while the AI interviewer asks follow-up questions. The resulting text data allow us to map democratic belief systems and to distinguish well-founded from superficial democratic attitudes. Your dissertation will be situated in the area of Computational Text Analysis for Mapping Democratic Belief Systems , and you can shape its specific focus within this area. Collect semi-structured interviews at large scale using AI Conversational Interviewing Analyze text data to describe and explain democratic belief systems Develop and carry out a PhD on Computational Text Analysis for Mapping Democratic Belief Systems Contribute to high-quality research publishing in leading venues Contribute innovative ideas for research hypotheses and designs Assist with project management and administrative tasks Your qualifications: Completed (or near completion of) M.A./M.Sc. in the Social Sciences, Psychology, Computer Science, Computational Linguistics, or a related field Computational social science skills, which are especially useful for this position: Python skills and experience working with LLMs (e.g., orchestrating LLM agents or building dialogue systems) and text-as-data methods and natural language processing Interest in or experience with political psychology, persuasion research, belief systems or democratic attitudes Strong organizational skills Good written and oral English communication skills; German language skills are an advantage for the experiments in the first project phase Willingness to work with the team on-site with flexible working hours and home office options Benefits: Involvement in innovative social science research with strong academic visibility, at the intersection of political science, psychology, and computational social science A 4-year contract with the possibility of extension that gives you the time to complete an ambitious dissertation A welcoming, inclusive atmosphere that highly values ambition, collaboration and individual contribution Supportive professional environment committed to your career growth Employment at one of Europe's premier research universities located in Munich, a city consistently ranked for outstanding quality of life Salary according to TV-L E13, 75% (approx. €44,000 to €47,000 gross per year depending on prior experience, non-binding, 1 , 2 ) Various services and benefits provided by LMU, including family support ( 1 , 2 ) People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: The application deadline is 20 October 2026, but we review applications on a rolling basis, so the earlier you apply, the better. Please send complete application documents, a writing sample (e.g. MA thesis or term paper) and a cover letter to A.Wuttke@lmu.de . Your cover letter should explain how your profile relates to the ideal profile outlined above and which questions about democratic belief systems you would like to pursue in your dissertation. Zoom interviews will begin in October 2026. Depending on the number of applications, a second stage with a work sample and in-person interview at LMU Munich may follow (travel costs covered). Join us in shaping democratic futures. Apply today! Where knowledge is everything. LMU researchers work at the highest level on the great questions affecting people, society, culture, the environment and technology — supported by experts in administration, IT and tech. Become part of LMU Munich! In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/7099e5d5250e30a7620e535ff3c70575eafc77060": {
      "title": "PhD Researcher (m/f/x)",
      "description": "PhD Researcher  (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4586 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Social Sciences - Geschwister-Scholl-Institute for Political Science, Unit Digitalization and Political Behavior (Prof. Dr. Alexander Wuttke) Remuneration group TV-L E13 Full-time / Part-time Part-time (75%) Start date 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible About us: At LMU Munich, the Unit Digitalization and Political Behavior (Prof. Dr. Alexander Wuttke) and Geschwister-Scholl Institute for Political Science are looking for a PhD Researcher. The position is fixed-term for 4 years, with the possibility of extension. We are looking for you: PhD Researcher  (m/f/x) in Munich Your tasks and responsibilities: Do you want to understand how ordinary people think about democracy? Do you want to run experiments and design interventions that foster support for democracy? Are you excited by the idea of using large language models and text data to answer questions that matter for society? If so, we warmly invite you to join our team. This position connects two DFG-funded research projects at our unit. Initially, you will work in the project \"Democratic Persuasion: How to Make the Case for Democracy\" ( Project Proposal ). You will continue in our Emmy Noether research group \"Predictably Paradoxical: Mapping the Democratic Mind with AI\" ( Project Proposal ). Drawing on psychological theory and collaborations between academia and civil society, Democratic Persuasion aims at strengthening democratic resilience in established democracies. We identify the motivations behind democratic skepticism and test which communicative strategies reinforce support for democracy. Our studies include survey experiments, LLM-based persuasive conversations, and a framed field experiment involving one-on-one dialogues. The Emmy Noether group seeks to illuminate the often puzzling and contradictory beliefs and attitudes people hold about democracy. Standardized surveys struggle to map what is on people's minds because their response options only capture what researchers already expect. The group therefore uses large language models as interviewers in semi-structured interviews. With AI Conversational Interviewing, respondents in many countries describe in their own words what democracy means to them, while the AI interviewer asks follow-up questions. The resulting text data allow us to map democratic belief systems and to distinguish well-founded from superficial democratic attitudes. Your dissertation will be situated in the area of Computational Text Analysis for Mapping Democratic Belief Systems , and you can shape its specific focus within this area. In Democratic Persuasion: Contribute to the framed field experiment on Socratic probing in collaboration with civil society organizations, from theory and research design to analysis Develop and implement your own theory-driven interventions to strengthen support for democracy Help conduct and analyze LLM-based persuasive conversations and survey experiments In the Emmy Noether group: Collect semi-structured interviews at large scale using AI Conversational Interviewing Analyze text data to describe and explain democratic belief systems Throughout: Develop and carry out a PhD on Computational Text Analysis for Mapping Democratic Belief Systems Contribute to high-quality research publishing in leading venues Contribute innovative ideas for research hypotheses and designs Assist with project management and administrative tasks Your qualifications: No candidate is expected to meet all the criteria listed below. The tasks are distributed among project members with diverse skill sets. Please explain in your cover letter how your profile relates to the following qualifications. Completed (or near completion of) M.A./M.Sc. in the Social Sciences, Psychology, Computer Science, Computational Linguistics, or a related field Computational social science skills, which are especially useful for this position: Python skills and experience working with LLMs (e.g., orchestrating LLM agents or building dialogue systems) and text-as-data methods and natural language processing Education in quantitative methodology, including experimental designs Interest in or experience with political psychology, persuasion research, belief systems or democratic attitudes Strong organizational skills Good written and oral English communication skills; German language skills are an advantage for the experiments in the first project phase Willingness to work with the team on-site with flexible working hours and home office options Benefits: Meaningful opportunities to foster democratic resilience through impactful research Involvement in innovative social science research with strong academic visibility, at the intersection of political science, psychology, and computational social science A 4-year contract with the possibility of extension that gives you the time to complete an ambitious dissertation Experience in two related research projects and a team with diverse methodological expertise A welcoming, inclusive atmosphere that highly values ambition, collaboration and individual contribution Supportive professional environment committed to your career growth Employment at one of Europe's premier research universities located in Munich, a city consistently ranked for outstanding quality of life Salary according to TV-L E13, 75% (approx. €44,000 to €47,000 gross per year depending on prior experience, non-binding, 1 , 2 ) Various services and benefits provided by LMU, including family support ( 1 , 2 ) People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: The application deadline is 20 October 2026, but we review applications on a rolling basis, so the earlier you apply, the better. Please send complete application documents, a writing sample (e.g. MA thesis or term paper) and a cover letter to A.Wuttke@lmu.de . Your cover letter should explain how your profile relates to the ideal profile outlined above and which questions about democratic belief systems you would like to pursue in your dissertation. Zoom interviews will begin in October 2026. Depending on the number of applications, a second stage with a work sample and in-person interview at LMU Munich may follow (travel costs covered). Join us in shaping democratic futures. Apply today! Where knowledge is everything. LMU researchers work at the highest level on the great questions affecting people, society, culture, the environment and technology — supported by experts in administration, IT and tech. Become part of LMU Munich! In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/b9702fe45f6630c2e4e1fbdc6fbd855b1e1837520": {
      "title": "PhD Student (m/f/x)",
      "description": "PhD Student (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4567 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Philosophy, Philosophy of Science and the Study of Religion - Chair of Metaphysics (Prof. Alyssa Ney) Remuneration group TV-L E13 Full-time / Part-time Part-time (65%) Start date 2027-10-01; limited to three years About us: The Chair of Metaphysics (Prof. Dr. Alyssa Ney) investigates metaphysical topics from an analytical perspective, aiming for an approach that is scientifically based, historically informed, and socially engaged. We are looking for you: PhD Student (m/f/x) in Munich Your tasks and responsibilities: The candidate will write a doctoral dissertation on metaphysical questions related to quantum physics under the supervision of Professor Alyssa Ney. They will also be expected to participate in the weekly group activities of the ERC project. Your qualifications: Candidates are required to have a masters degree in philosophy or philosophy of science, with a strong background in philosophy of physics and metaphysics. Additional background training in physics is welcome. All work will be conducted in English. Benefits: The Chair of Metaphysics in the Faculty of Philosophy at LMU Munich invites applications for one PhD position in a European Research Council supported project on the metaphysics of quantum physics. The starting date is October 1, 2027. Remuneration is based on the TV-L E13 pay scale (65%). The Chair of Metaphysics is a vibrant center of research at LMU Munich, which hosts regular workshops, a colloquium series, and other events through the academic year. Prospective applicants can learn more about the Chair by visiting here . Applications from women and members of underrepresented groups are expressly welcome. People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: Applications should be written entirely in English and include the following: letter of motivation CV with certificates writing sample (e.g a master’s thesis) statement of research interests (maximum three pages) the names and e-mail addresses of three professors who can send letters of recommendation, if requested. These materials should be compiled into a single PDF and sent by December 15, 2026 at the latest to: office.ney@lrz.uni-muenchen.de . The e-mail subject line should include \"ERC PhD student application\" and the applicant's name. Where knowledge is everything. LMU researchers work at the highest level on the great questions affecting people, society, culture, the environment and technology — supported by experts in administration, IT and tech. Become part of LMU Munich! In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/18b5a5e9fb292b3c0154c0c6d77bfa17df7b45ca0": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher (m/f/x)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher  (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4587 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Social Sciences - Geschwister-Scholl-Institute for Political Science, Unit Digitalization and Political Behavior (Prof. Dr. Alexander Wuttke) Remuneration group TV-L E13 Full-time / Part-time Full-time (100%) Start date 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible About us: At LMU Munich, the Unit Digitalization and Political Behavior (Prof. Dr. Alexander Wuttke) and Geschwister-Scholl Institute for Political Science are looking for a Postdoctoral Researcher. The position is fixed-term until 31 October 2028; extension possible conditional on funding availabilities. We are looking for you: Postdoctoral Researcher  (m/f/x) in Munich Your tasks and responsibilities: Are you keen to understand why democracy faces growing challenges and eager to develop meaningful countermeasures at the level of individual citizens? Do you thrive on combining rigorous academic research with tangible societal impact? If so, we warmly invite you to join our team. As a key member of the DFG-funded research project \"Democratic Persuasion: How to Make the Case for Democracy\" ( Project Proposal ), you will be part of a collaborative effort between academia and civil society aimed at strengthening democratic resilience in established democracies. We identify the motivations behind democratic skepticism and test which communicative strategies can win back citizens whose support for democracy is wavering. Our studies include survey experiments, AI-assisted persuasive conversations, and field experiments with group discussions and one-on-one dialogues. In this role, you will lead the project's framed field experiment, which we run together with civil society organizations. It tests Socratic probing, a conversation technique that invites people to examine the reasons behind their own beliefs, as a way to reduce anti-democratic attitudes. Beyond this study, you will develop your own theory-driven interventions for strengthening democratic support and put them to the test, using LLM-based persuasive conversations or survey experiments. The contract ends with the project in October 2028. Since the project is already up and running, you can concentrate on running studies and publishing from your first weeks. Lead the framed field experiment on Socratic probing in collaboration with civil society organizations, from design and pre-registration to analysis Develop and implement your own theory-driven interventions to strengthen support for democracy Implement LLM-based persuasive conversations and/or persuasive survey experiments Publish high-quality research in leading venues Coordinate project management and administrative tasks Your qualifications: Importantly, no candidate is expected to meet all the criteria listed below. The tasks are distributed among project members with diverse skill sets. Please explain in your cover letter how your profile relates to the following qualifications. Completed (or near completion of) PhD in political science, psychology, communication science, or a related field Substantive expertise related to the project: research in political psychology and persuasion research and/or research on democratic attitudes, democratic backsliding, or related topics Methodological expertise in field experiments and/or survey experiments Computational social science skills, in particular orchestrating LLM agents in Python Proficiency in R or Python for data wrangling and analysis Experience with Open Science practices and commitment to research integrity Strong organizational and project management skills Good written and oral English communication skills; German language skills are an advantage, as the experiments take place in Germany Willingness to work with the team on-site with flexible working hours and home office options Benefits: Meaningful opportunities to foster democratic resilience through impactful research A leading role in innovative experimental research with strong academic visibility Freedom to develop and test your own intervention ideas A welcoming, inclusive atmosphere that highly values collaboration and individual contribution A research environment shared with our DFG Emmy Noether group, which studies democratic belief systems with AI Conversational Interviewing Dedicated personal budget for research activities and conference travel Employment at one of Europe's premier research universities located in Munich, a city consistently ranked for outstanding quality of life Salary according to TV-L E13, full-time (approx. €66,000 to €72,000 gross per year depending on prior experience, non-binding, 1 ) Generous postdoctoral support and development opportunities ( 1 , 2 ) Various services and benefits provided by LMU, including family support ( 1 , 2 ) Also possible in a part-time capacity. People with disabilities who are equally as qualified as other applicants will receive preferential treatment. Contact: The application deadline is 20 October 2026, but we review applications on a rolling basis, so the earlier you apply, the better. Please send complete application documents, a writing sample (e.g., a publication or a pre-registration plan) and a cover letter to A.Wuttke@lmu.de . Your cover letter should explain how your profile relates to the ideal profile outlined above. Please also sketch, in a few sentences, an intervention to strengthen support for democracy that you would like to test. Zoom interviews will begin in October 2026. Depending on the number of applications, a second stage with a work sample and in-person interview at LMU Munich may follow (travel costs covered). Join us in shaping democratic futures. Apply today! Where knowledge is everything. LMU researchers work at the highest level on the great questions affecting people, society, culture, the environment and technology — supported by experts in administration, IT and tech. Become part of LMU Munich! In the course of your application for an open position at Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität (LMU) München, you will be required to submit personal information. Please be sure to refer to our LMU Privacy Policy . By submitting your application, you confirm that you have read and understood our data protection guidelines and privacy policy and that you agree to your data being processed in accordance with the selection process. Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 80539 München www.lmu.de Imprint Accessibility Back to the LMU Job Portal"
    },
    "https://job-portal.lmu.de/jobposting/a1aa5c6feae1cb87dba986e8e528defc366390270": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher (m/f/x)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher (m/f/x) | Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München | 4481 Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München is a leading research university in Europe. Since its founding in 1472 it has been committed to the highest international standards of excellence in research and teaching. Institution Faculty of Philosophy, Philosophy of Science and the Study of Religion - Chair of Metaphysics (Prof. Dr. Alyssa Ney) Remuneration group TV-L E13 Full-time / Part-time Full-time (100%) Start date 2027-01-15; limited to two years About us: The Chair of Metaphysics (Prof. Dr. Alyssa Ney) investigates metaphysical topics from an analytical perspective, aiming for an approach that is scientifically based, historically informed, and socially engaged. We are looking for you: Postdoctoral Researcher (m/f/x) in Munich Your tasks and responsibilities: The candidate will be expected to maintain an active research program in metaphysics and philosophy of physics, including issues related to the metaphysics of quantum open systems. They will also be expected to participate in the weekly group activities of the Chair of Metaphysics. The position will include some administrative tasks, such as the organization of workshops. Your qualifications: Candidates are required to have a doctorate in philosophy, with an AOS in Philosophy of Physics and Metaphysics. Additional background training in physics is welcome. This is a research-only position, and all work will be conducted in English. Benefits: The Chair of Metaphysics in the Faculty of Philosophy at LMU Munich invites applications for one postdoc position in a multi-university John Templeton Foundation supported project on the physics and philosophy of open quantum systems. This is a fixed term (non-tenure track) postdoctoral position that runs for two years. The starting date is January 15, 2027. Remuneration is based on the TV-L E13 pay scale (100%). The Chair of Metaphysics is a vibrant center of research at 